WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.930
A four digit odometer reads three, four, four, three.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:05.370
It is a palindrome.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.030
Imagine putting a mirror between the middle wheels.

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:09.920
Which wheels can you choose freely,

00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:11.770
and which are already determined?

00:00:12.920 --> 00:00:14.670
The first two wheels show thirty four.

00:00:15.620 --> 00:00:17.320
Reflect them in reverse order to make thirty

00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:18.710
four, forty three.

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:22.510
Advancing the first pair to thirty five produces three,

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.110
five, five, three.

00:00:24.500 --> 00:00:26.730
The palindromes are a hundred and ten apart.

00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:31.350
Now the odometer shows three, nine, nine, three.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:33.130
Predict the next palindrome.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.610
Be careful when the nine rolls back to zero.

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.810
The next possible first pair is forty.

00:00:42.460 --> 00:00:46.160
Its reflection is zero four, so the new reading is four

00:00:46.160 --> 00:00:47.270
thousand and four.

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:49.990
Subtract three thousand nine hundred and ninety three.

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:53.030
This time the gap is just eleven.

00:00:53.860 --> 00:00:56.084
From one thousand and one through nine thousand nine

00:00:56.084 --> 00:00:59.460
hundred and ninety nine, how many whole numbers lie

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:01.860
exactly halfway between two consecutive

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:04.080
four digit palindromes?

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.470
Count the gaps, without listing every address.

00:01:10.310 --> 00:01:11.980
Hold the thousands wheel fixed.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.800
The hundreds wheel runs from zero to nine.

00:01:15.130 --> 00:01:17.580
Ten palindromes make nine gaps of one hundred and ten.

00:01:18.670 --> 00:01:20.770
Each even gap has one whole number exactly

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:21.780
in its middle.

00:01:22.210 --> 00:01:24.700
There are nine possible nonzero thousands digits.

00:01:25.830 --> 00:01:28.880
Nine groups of nine give eighty one halfway addresses.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:31.750
The eight carries between the groups each make a

00:01:31.750 --> 00:01:32.500
gap of eleven.

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:35.160
Half of eleven is not a whole number,

00:01:35.510 --> 00:01:37.130
so those carries contribute none.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:40.660
Eighty one large gaps and eight small ones give eighty

00:01:40.660 --> 00:01:43.180
nine gaps among ninety palindromes.

00:01:43.910 --> 00:01:47.260
A mirror makes the last two digits follow the first two.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:50.830
To find the next palindrome, increase the first pair and

00:01:50.830 --> 00:01:51.720
reflect it again.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.650
When counting a pattern, test the change at a carry

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.840
before assuming every step is equal.
